00:02:03.495 --> 00:02:04.870
So let's start by
briefly talking

00:02:04.870 --> 00:02:08.020
about what the actual challenges
that we want to solve here.

00:02:08.020 --> 00:02:10.090
I think we kind of
all roughly know

00:02:10.090 --> 00:02:12.640
deployment is all about
moving fast without breaking

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:14.410
too many things.

00:02:14.410 --> 00:02:16.780
So we want to roll out new
versions of applications.

00:02:16.780 --> 00:02:18.880
We want to do that safely.

00:02:18.880 --> 00:02:19.940
Should be easy.

00:02:19.940 --> 00:02:22.960
We don't want people to have to
follow a 12-step cube-ctl guide

00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:24.300
or whatever.

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:26.080
It should be reliable.

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:28.270
Ideally, it works
well when you do it,

00:02:28.270 --> 00:02:31.720
but also if something doesn't
quite work as expected,

00:02:31.720 --> 00:02:34.760
you should have good
fallback options.

00:02:34.760 --> 00:02:36.430
And then, of course
in an ideal world

00:02:36.430 --> 00:02:38.020
we want it to be
aligned with whatever

00:02:38.020 --> 00:02:39.700
is considered best practice.

00:02:39.700 --> 00:02:41.800
And if we're talking a
Kubernetes environment,

00:02:41.800 --> 00:02:43.900
a lot of that revolves
around how can we

00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:47.170
do this in a declarative way,
where we focus on our intent

00:02:47.170 --> 00:02:49.630
rather than exactly
the details of how

00:02:49.630 --> 00:02:52.250
we're trying to roll that out.

00:02:52.250 --> 00:02:54.530
So just high level,
what's the scenario

00:02:54.530 --> 00:02:55.780
that we're talking about here?

00:02:55.780 --> 00:02:57.280
Nothing too particularly fancy.

00:02:57.280 --> 00:03:00.617
And in general, I
should say, the main aim

00:03:00.617 --> 00:03:03.200
from my perspective, we're not
going to talk about magic here.

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:04.940
Most of you may
know all the things

00:03:04.940 --> 00:03:06.320
that we're talking about here.

00:03:06.320 --> 00:03:08.300
It's more about
representing a pattern

00:03:08.300 --> 00:03:10.340
so that you can take
away with some guidance

00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:11.875
and some confidence.

00:03:11.875 --> 00:03:14.000
But the overall scenario
is the [INAUDIBLE] version

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:17.030
x of my application
is running somewhere.

00:03:17.030 --> 00:03:19.160
We'll assume it's
serving web traffic.

00:03:19.160 --> 00:03:20.750
Most of what we're
going to talk about

00:03:20.750 --> 00:03:22.460
applies equally
well to applications

00:03:22.460 --> 00:03:25.170
that do things like processing
messages off a cube.

00:03:25.170 --> 00:03:26.690
But for the purpose
of the diagrams

00:03:26.690 --> 00:03:28.190
and so on, we'll
assume that there's

00:03:28.190 --> 00:03:30.710
a web serving application.

00:03:30.710 --> 00:03:33.080
And then you've got a new
version, y, coming out of your

00:03:33.080 --> 00:03:35.810
build process or
your CI process,

00:03:35.810 --> 00:03:39.360
and you want to switch users
over to that application.

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:42.200
So at a high level, the
sort of simple diagram

00:03:42.200 --> 00:03:44.420
is you've got a load
balancer of some sort,

00:03:44.420 --> 00:03:46.462
you've got a running
version of your application,

00:03:46.462 --> 00:03:49.490
v1 in this case, magic happens.

00:03:49.490 --> 00:03:52.160
Everything is safe and
reliable and wonderful.

00:03:52.160 --> 00:03:53.690
Tah-dah!

00:03:53.690 --> 00:03:56.480
We finish with version 2
of my application running.

00:03:56.480 --> 00:03:58.090
That's really, in
a nutshell, all

00:03:58.090 --> 00:03:59.840
that we're going to
talk about, how to try

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:02.930
to make this work really well.

00:04:02.930 --> 00:04:03.890
So a quick recap.

00:04:03.890 --> 00:04:06.280
What does Kubernetes already
give you out of the box?

00:04:06.280 --> 00:04:07.750
Most of you are
probably familiar

00:04:07.750 --> 00:04:10.330
with the deployment
object, specifically,

00:04:10.330 --> 00:04:12.310
if you look at
the rolling update

00:04:12.310 --> 00:04:14.968
strategy of the
deployment object,

00:04:14.968 --> 00:04:17.260
there's already a pretty good
option for out of the box

00:04:17.260 --> 00:04:21.029
magic, if you like, as we
just saw in that slide.

00:04:21.029 --> 00:04:23.020
And what you get with
a deployment object

00:04:23.020 --> 00:04:26.080
is what we might call a
rolling blue green cutover.

00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:28.910
Talk about that a
little bit in a second.

00:04:28.910 --> 00:04:31.060
And there are also
some useful controls

00:04:31.060 --> 00:04:34.510
that you have to handle
how much over-provisioning

00:04:34.510 --> 00:04:36.790
of new resources you want,
and how quickly you want

00:04:36.790 --> 00:04:39.270
the old resources to
die away, specifically,

00:04:39.270 --> 00:04:42.550
there's two parameters, max
surge and max unavailable,

00:04:42.550 --> 00:04:44.017
that you can use to tune this.

00:04:44.017 --> 00:04:45.850
So there's already some
pretty nice options.

00:04:45.850 --> 00:04:48.670
And what you get is a picture
that looks a bit like this.

00:04:48.670 --> 00:04:49.793
You've got service.

00:04:49.793 --> 00:04:51.460
You've got your
deployment object, which

00:04:51.460 --> 00:04:52.575
is managing a replica set.

00:04:52.575 --> 00:04:54.680
It starts out here.

00:04:54.680 --> 00:04:58.700
You decide to switch to v2,
spins up any replica set,

00:04:58.700 --> 00:05:07.640
adds new pods, more another
one, dot, dot, dot, finished.

00:05:07.640 --> 00:05:08.192
Great.

00:05:08.192 --> 00:05:09.900
So that's what you
can do out of the box.

00:05:09.900 --> 00:05:12.330
And the parameters control
how many new ones you spin up,

00:05:12.330 --> 00:05:14.820
how quickly the
old ones go away.

00:05:14.820 --> 00:05:16.050
So what's good about this?

00:05:16.050 --> 00:05:19.030
Well, I guess the best thing--
it works out of the box.

00:05:19.030 --> 00:05:21.480
You can pretty much do this
without any real effort.

00:05:21.480 --> 00:05:23.130
You have stable
naming, which is nice.

00:05:23.130 --> 00:05:25.920
It means that the resource
name, if you like,

00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:28.590
that your application
workload refers to,

00:05:28.590 --> 00:05:31.092
is the same like even v1, v2.

00:05:31.092 --> 00:05:32.550
If that's not part
of the name, you

00:05:32.550 --> 00:05:33.842
can just pull in your workload.

00:05:33.842 --> 00:05:36.030
And you don't need to
do anything low level

00:05:36.030 --> 00:05:37.900
like managing labels.

00:05:37.900 --> 00:05:39.090
It just works.

00:05:39.090 --> 00:05:41.070
But there's also
some limitations

00:05:41.070 --> 00:05:41.970
that come with this.

00:05:41.970 --> 00:05:46.660
And when I say limitations,
I should be clear.

00:05:46.660 --> 00:05:49.740
This can be perfectly fine
for 80%, 90%, maybe 100%

00:05:49.740 --> 00:05:50.538
of your use cases.

00:05:50.538 --> 00:05:52.080
So just because
there are some things

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:54.090
you can't do with a
deployment object,

00:05:54.090 --> 00:05:56.910
absolutely doesn't mean that
it might not be good enough

00:05:56.910 --> 00:05:58.840
for everything that you need.

00:05:58.840 --> 00:06:01.290
But for example, one thing
that is not that easy to do

00:06:01.290 --> 00:06:04.732
is when one of your
new versions comes up,

00:06:04.732 --> 00:06:06.690
you can't really get in
there and manipulate it

00:06:06.690 --> 00:06:08.670
very well before it
actually gets traffic.

00:06:08.670 --> 00:06:10.440
You can play a bit
with the health check,

00:06:10.440 --> 00:06:11.815
but it's not that
easy to control

00:06:11.815 --> 00:06:14.590
that in a fine grained way.

00:06:14.590 --> 00:06:16.177
The traffic cutover process--

00:06:16.177 --> 00:06:18.510
it's kind of baked into what
the deployment object does.

00:06:18.510 --> 00:06:21.390
Like, as the new pods become
healthy, they get traffic.

00:06:21.390 --> 00:06:24.990
You don't have a lot of fine
grained control about that.

00:06:24.990 --> 00:06:26.795
And also in terms
of fallback options,

00:06:26.795 --> 00:06:28.420
like, once the
deployment has happened,

00:06:28.420 --> 00:06:31.005
and if you don't like
the way v2 is behaving,

00:06:31.005 --> 00:06:33.480
you kind of got to do
the process in reverse

00:06:33.480 --> 00:06:34.277
to go back to v1.

00:06:34.277 --> 00:06:36.360
You can get the old revision,
but for example, you

00:06:36.360 --> 00:06:39.330
don't have a running
instance that you can quickly

00:06:39.330 --> 00:06:42.320
switch traffic back to.

00:06:42.320 --> 00:06:45.270
So if we need some
more flexibility here,

00:06:45.270 --> 00:06:48.240
we can do some things that are
a little bit more fine grained

00:06:48.240 --> 00:06:50.335
than what the deployment
object provides.

00:06:50.335 --> 00:06:52.710
And so we're trading a little
bit more effort on our part

00:06:52.710 --> 00:06:56.580
to actually make this work
with more flexibility and more

00:06:56.580 --> 00:06:58.540
options that we have.

00:06:58.540 --> 00:07:01.620
So as an example, we can do
a full blue green deployment.

00:07:01.620 --> 00:07:03.230
I'll show a diagram
in a second, which

00:07:03.230 --> 00:07:05.070
is not a rolling
blue green, where

00:07:05.070 --> 00:07:07.480
we get the whole new
application running.

00:07:07.480 --> 00:07:09.250
And then we can do
things with that

00:07:09.250 --> 00:07:11.850
and then we can
switch traffic over.

00:07:11.850 --> 00:07:14.460
For example, we can use that
as a pattern to carry out

00:07:14.460 --> 00:07:17.580
more extensive validation
of the new workload

00:07:17.580 --> 00:07:20.900
before we decide to
expose our users to it.

00:07:20.900 --> 00:07:23.840
So a high level might
look something like this.

00:07:23.840 --> 00:07:25.670
So you've got a load balancer.

00:07:25.670 --> 00:07:29.300
You've got your current version
of your application running.

00:07:29.300 --> 00:07:30.880
You spin up the new version.

00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:32.630
So now you've got this
thing sitting there

00:07:32.630 --> 00:07:35.090
in a sort of dark launch
pattern where it's not

00:07:35.090 --> 00:07:38.900
getting any traffic yet, but the
app is running, it's warmed up,

00:07:38.900 --> 00:07:41.000
you can run things against it.

00:07:41.000 --> 00:07:43.160
You decide it's good to
switch your traffic over.

00:07:43.160 --> 00:07:45.980
Now your users are
using the new version,

00:07:45.980 --> 00:07:48.170
but you still have the
old version around,

00:07:48.170 --> 00:07:49.760
which is useful.

00:07:49.760 --> 00:07:52.250
Imagine your apps take
a while to boot up.

00:07:52.250 --> 00:07:54.980
If there's a problem now, you
can easily switch traffic back

00:07:54.980 --> 00:07:58.437
to the old version, and
those apps will serve traffic

00:07:58.437 --> 00:07:59.020
straight away.

00:08:01.907 --> 00:08:03.740
You wait and then you
can have this standing

00:08:03.740 --> 00:08:04.370
around for a while.

00:08:04.370 --> 00:08:06.440
And eventually, when you're
confident that the new version

00:08:06.440 --> 00:08:08.273
really is good, then
of course, you probably

00:08:08.273 --> 00:08:09.923
want to get rid of
the old resources

00:08:09.923 --> 00:08:12.090
so that you don't have them
standing around forever.

00:08:14.695 --> 00:08:16.570
I actually have some
wonderful slides-- well,

00:08:16.570 --> 00:08:18.910
I think wonderful-- that talk
about how to do this in detail

00:08:18.910 --> 00:08:19.577
with Kubernetes.

00:08:19.577 --> 00:08:22.060
We don't have, I think, enough
time to cover them today.

00:08:22.060 --> 00:08:26.360
If you're interested, please
come up and see me afterwards.

00:08:26.360 --> 00:08:28.068
So that gives us one pattern.

00:08:28.068 --> 00:08:29.860
But if we need even
more safety than this--

00:08:29.860 --> 00:08:34.096
so there's a few things that
can go wrong with this pattern--

00:08:34.096 --> 00:08:36.429
we can use that blue green
pattern that we talked about.

00:08:36.429 --> 00:08:37.971
And instead of just
switching traffic

00:08:37.971 --> 00:08:39.610
over when the new
app is running,

00:08:39.610 --> 00:08:41.840
we can decide to
perform some more tests.

00:08:41.840 --> 00:08:43.840
We can run a suite of
integration or performance

00:08:43.840 --> 00:08:47.380
tests, or even allow a
set of limited users--

00:08:47.380 --> 00:08:49.690
our internal friends
or maybe a set of ERP

00:08:49.690 --> 00:08:53.800
customers to hit the
new version of the app.

00:08:53.800 --> 00:08:59.770
We can do a sort of manual very,
or a very slow rolling update

00:08:59.770 --> 00:09:00.560
if you like.

00:09:00.560 --> 00:09:02.320
So if you go back
to the picture,

00:09:02.320 --> 00:09:05.170
rather than bringing up
all the new instances,

00:09:05.170 --> 00:09:07.300
we can bring up a few.

00:09:07.300 --> 00:09:08.800
And then we can
switch traffic over.

00:09:08.800 --> 00:09:11.690
And we can slowly,
slowly increase

00:09:11.690 --> 00:09:14.050
the amount of instances
that serve traffic,

00:09:14.050 --> 00:09:18.182
and slowly, slowly
decrease the old version.

00:09:18.182 --> 00:09:20.140
It's basically what the
deployment object does,

00:09:20.140 --> 00:09:24.280
but we can control the stages
in which that happens, or we

00:09:24.280 --> 00:09:28.930
can do a canary roll up,
which is another pattern that

00:09:28.930 --> 00:09:33.110
allows us to inject some more
safety into this process.

00:09:33.110 --> 00:09:37.060
So I think most of you will
have heard of this term before.

00:09:37.060 --> 00:09:40.030
I'll define it for the
purposes of this discussion.

00:09:40.030 --> 00:09:41.530
What is a canary roll out?

00:09:41.530 --> 00:09:44.350
Well, it's a way of evaluating
a candidate version, which

00:09:44.350 --> 00:09:48.260
is the new version we want to
deploy, against the baseline,

00:09:48.260 --> 00:09:50.740
which is related to the
currently running version most

00:09:50.740 --> 00:09:53.800
of the time, to check
for unexpected deviations

00:09:53.800 --> 00:09:55.735
of behavior.

00:09:55.735 --> 00:09:58.360
So this is not about functional
testing or something like that.

00:09:58.360 --> 00:10:00.790
This is not about checking
whether the new version does

00:10:00.790 --> 00:10:02.143
what you want it to do.

00:10:02.143 --> 00:10:04.810
You have a new feature, you want
to make sure the feature works.

00:10:04.810 --> 00:10:09.010
No, it's about verifying
that the new version behaves

00:10:09.010 --> 00:10:12.520
acceptably, whatever that means,
with respect to the existing

00:10:12.520 --> 00:10:13.250
version.

00:10:13.250 --> 00:10:15.130
So for example,
the CPU utilization

00:10:15.130 --> 00:10:19.150
doesn't go through the roof
or something like that.

00:10:19.150 --> 00:10:22.020
And this is useful if the
application's behavior

00:10:22.020 --> 00:10:24.640
across the metrics
that you're measuring

00:10:24.640 --> 00:10:26.377
is not expected to change a lot.

00:10:26.377 --> 00:10:27.960
Of course, there are
types of changes.

00:10:27.960 --> 00:10:30.450
Imagine you've added
the CPU fix where you're

00:10:30.450 --> 00:10:32.850
expecting the
application's performance

00:10:32.850 --> 00:10:35.220
to behave quite differently.

00:10:35.220 --> 00:10:38.010
And so as part of your
analysis of your canary

00:10:38.010 --> 00:10:40.365
to figure out whether you
should go ahead or not,

00:10:40.365 --> 00:10:41.990
you need to be able
to take these kinds

00:10:41.990 --> 00:10:44.190
of expected deviations
into account as well.

00:10:44.190 --> 00:10:48.120
And Nora is going to talk
a little bit about some

00:10:48.120 --> 00:10:51.690
of the things that you can
do in your canary analysis

00:10:51.690 --> 00:10:53.450
to make this work.

00:10:53.450 --> 00:10:56.160
So what's the schematic
for a canary rollout?

00:10:56.160 --> 00:10:58.030
Well, you start with
a load balancer.

00:10:58.030 --> 00:11:00.450
You've got your
current app running.

00:11:00.450 --> 00:11:02.760
And then you move to
this intermediate state

00:11:02.760 --> 00:11:05.010
in which you have your
current running app

00:11:05.010 --> 00:11:08.050
and you have a baseline
and you have a candidate.

00:11:08.050 --> 00:11:09.690
Why the baseline?

00:11:09.690 --> 00:11:12.960
It's very common to see what
I might call simple canary

00:11:12.960 --> 00:11:15.420
approaches where you just
compare the candidates

00:11:15.420 --> 00:11:17.640
with the existing version.

00:11:17.640 --> 00:11:21.650
That's OK, but it's really not
an apples to apples comparison.

00:11:21.650 --> 00:11:23.870
To take a simple
example, the baseline

00:11:23.870 --> 00:11:26.190
or the original version is
serving much more traffic

00:11:26.190 --> 00:11:27.280
than the candidate.

00:11:27.280 --> 00:11:29.580
So it might behave differently
just because of that.

00:11:29.580 --> 00:11:31.050
It's also been
running for however

00:11:31.050 --> 00:11:33.300
long it's been running,
whereas the candidate has just

00:11:33.300 --> 00:11:34.930
booted up.

00:11:34.930 --> 00:11:38.520
And so if you want a proper
side by side evaluation,

00:11:38.520 --> 00:11:41.850
you should have a baseline
that matches the candidate

00:11:41.850 --> 00:11:42.960
as closely as possible.

00:11:42.960 --> 00:11:46.105
And that means it should serve
the same percentage of traffic,

00:11:46.105 --> 00:11:48.480
and it should have been spun
up and configured in roughly

00:11:48.480 --> 00:11:51.030
the same way as the candidate.

00:11:51.030 --> 00:11:54.030
So you run this
for a while and you

00:11:54.030 --> 00:11:55.380
decide if you want to go ahead.

00:11:55.380 --> 00:11:59.790
Great, you can get rid of the
baseline in the old version

00:11:59.790 --> 00:12:03.280
and just switch all the
traffic to your new version.

00:12:03.280 --> 00:12:07.470
And I'm glossing over how that
evaluation process happens.

00:12:07.470 --> 00:12:10.080
Nora is going to talk a lot
more about some tips and tricks

00:12:10.080 --> 00:12:11.430
about how to get that right.

00:12:11.430 --> 00:12:14.790
Or if it doesn't work
out, you decide OK,

00:12:14.790 --> 00:12:16.410
that wasn't a good idea.

00:12:16.410 --> 00:12:17.280
Goodbye baseline.

00:12:17.280 --> 00:12:18.260
Goodbye candidate.

00:12:18.260 --> 00:12:20.760
And then we're back to serving
traffic with the old version,

00:12:20.760 --> 00:12:23.290
and we try again.

00:12:23.290 --> 00:12:25.880
So how do we do this
with Kubernetes vanilla?

00:12:25.880 --> 00:12:28.340
We can use label
selectors on the service.

00:12:28.340 --> 00:12:31.280
And we can use appropriate
sizing of our baseline

00:12:31.280 --> 00:12:34.087
in our candidate to
approximate the traffic

00:12:34.087 --> 00:12:35.170
distribution that we want.

00:12:38.283 --> 00:12:39.950
We could use replica
sets, for instance,

00:12:39.950 --> 00:12:41.950
but we would recommend
using a deployment object

00:12:41.950 --> 00:12:43.870
for the workload
just because that's

00:12:43.870 --> 00:12:47.170
the thing Kubernetes uses
for these kind of workloads.

00:12:47.170 --> 00:12:49.570
And for consistency, with
the way the common deployment

00:12:49.570 --> 00:12:53.230
object works, we recommend that
if you do decide to go ahead,

00:12:53.230 --> 00:12:55.480
instead of switching traffic
over to your candidate,

00:12:55.480 --> 00:12:58.180
you actually just update
the existing deployment

00:12:58.180 --> 00:12:59.290
object in place.

00:12:59.290 --> 00:13:02.440
So that way it looks just
like a regular update if you

00:13:02.440 --> 00:13:04.560
weren't using canary analysis.

00:13:04.560 --> 00:13:07.060
So what's the schematic if we're
doing this with Kubernetes?

00:13:07.060 --> 00:13:08.500
We have a service.

00:13:08.500 --> 00:13:09.870
We have a deployment.

00:13:09.870 --> 00:13:11.500
And we have some
labeled selectors

00:13:11.500 --> 00:13:14.810
that are pointing at
the current workload.

00:13:14.810 --> 00:13:16.240
I'm using the conventional ones.

00:13:16.240 --> 00:13:17.390
They're useful.

00:13:17.390 --> 00:13:18.970
You might want to use your own.

00:13:18.970 --> 00:13:22.180
That's not a hard and
fast recommendation.

00:13:22.180 --> 00:13:25.180
Notice that the label selector
here is quite specific.

00:13:25.180 --> 00:13:27.280
And you'll see in
a second why it

00:13:27.280 --> 00:13:31.030
has this component equal
server additional selector,

00:13:31.030 --> 00:13:33.970
because as we move into
the intermediate stage,

00:13:33.970 --> 00:13:36.640
where we have three
deployments all of a sudden,

00:13:36.640 --> 00:13:38.980
we obviously have to loosen
the label selector so

00:13:38.980 --> 00:13:42.160
that it hits all three.

00:13:42.160 --> 00:13:43.570
Label selectors
on services don't

00:13:43.570 --> 00:13:44.980
support the set-based selector.

00:13:44.980 --> 00:13:47.116
So that's how we're
going to do this.

00:13:47.116 --> 00:13:52.750
And now we have traffic roughly
distributed across the three

00:13:52.750 --> 00:13:53.670
different deployments.

00:13:53.670 --> 00:13:55.450
And so that's where
the sizing comes in.

00:13:55.450 --> 00:13:57.150
We have six and one and one.

00:13:57.150 --> 00:13:59.590
And that gives us roughly
this distribution.

00:13:59.590 --> 00:14:01.090
I say roughly,
because that's really

00:14:01.090 --> 00:14:03.730
heavily dependent on the
characteristics of your load

00:14:03.730 --> 00:14:05.480
balancer of your service.

00:14:05.480 --> 00:14:09.460
So don't assume just because
the math of the sizing

00:14:09.460 --> 00:14:10.350
works out like this.

00:14:10.350 --> 00:14:11.850
But that's what
you're going to get.

00:14:11.850 --> 00:14:14.530
If you want to do vanilla
canary with Kubernetes,

00:14:14.530 --> 00:14:17.253
we highly recommend that you run
some experiments to figure out

00:14:17.253 --> 00:14:19.420
whether you're actually
getting this kind of traffic

00:14:19.420 --> 00:14:21.280
distribution, because
load balances have

00:14:21.280 --> 00:14:25.010
lots of intelligence built in
that might make this different.

00:14:25.010 --> 00:14:26.410
I should say one quick thing.

00:14:26.410 --> 00:14:30.460
One thing that you need in order
to spin up the baseline here,

00:14:30.460 --> 00:14:33.130
you need access to the
deployment configuration

00:14:33.130 --> 00:14:35.270
of your current app.

00:14:35.270 --> 00:14:37.090
And so that means that
you have to get that

00:14:37.090 --> 00:14:39.310
either from the runtime
or from a GitOps repo

00:14:39.310 --> 00:14:40.720
or whatever you have.

00:14:40.720 --> 00:14:42.460
It's not just the
new deployment object

00:14:42.460 --> 00:14:43.950
that you need to speed this up.

00:14:43.950 --> 00:14:44.450
All right.

00:14:44.450 --> 00:14:45.380
Need to speed up here.

00:14:45.380 --> 00:14:47.260
So we decide to go ahead, great.

00:14:47.260 --> 00:14:49.480
We narrow down the
label selector again.

00:14:49.480 --> 00:14:51.110
We update the new version.

00:14:51.110 --> 00:14:54.010
It does its rollout, one,
two, three, wait, done.

00:14:54.010 --> 00:14:54.880
Wonderful.

00:14:54.880 --> 00:14:59.670
So now we have our canary
deployment finished.

00:14:59.670 --> 00:15:02.100
So if we need more
sophistication, this works.

00:15:02.100 --> 00:15:04.190
And you can do vanilla
canary with Kubernetes

00:15:04.190 --> 00:15:05.793
in this pattern.

00:15:05.793 --> 00:15:07.460
But if we need some
more sophistication,

00:15:07.460 --> 00:15:09.520
we noticed there
were already some--

00:15:09.520 --> 00:15:10.570
well, a little bit of--

00:15:10.570 --> 00:15:13.160
so I wouldn't say friction,
but the sizing was already

00:15:13.160 --> 00:15:16.130
a bit of a challenge, we can use
Istio to do more things here.

00:15:16.130 --> 00:15:17.365
So here's a service mesh.

00:15:17.365 --> 00:15:18.240
Heard a lot about it.

00:15:18.240 --> 00:15:20.270
It has many more
features and many more

00:15:20.270 --> 00:15:23.000
use cases than just
traffic distribution

00:15:23.000 --> 00:15:24.890
in a canary rollout.

00:15:24.890 --> 00:15:26.840
But it's a very
useful thing to use

00:15:26.840 --> 00:15:30.098
if you're interested in doing a
more sophisticated canary too.

00:15:30.098 --> 00:15:31.640
So there's two main
things that we're

00:15:31.640 --> 00:15:33.650
going to talk about
today that are useful,

00:15:33.650 --> 00:15:36.830
one is that it supports
fine grained traffic splits,

00:15:36.830 --> 00:15:39.110
and the other, that you
can do content based

00:15:39.110 --> 00:15:40.990
rooting in your canary as well.

00:15:40.990 --> 00:15:43.180
I think that's a bit
of an advance topic.

00:15:43.180 --> 00:15:45.555
It's not that everyone has to
go away and implement that,

00:15:45.555 --> 00:15:47.340
but it's nice to know
that it's possible.

00:15:47.340 --> 00:15:49.700
So there's two Istio
resources that we

00:15:49.700 --> 00:15:52.840
need to know about if we're
going to use it like this.

00:15:52.840 --> 00:15:54.600
There's the destination rule.

00:15:54.600 --> 00:15:55.720
And this defines subset.

00:15:55.720 --> 00:15:59.060
So that's resources to which
traffic should be sent.

00:15:59.060 --> 00:16:01.070
And then there's
the virtual service,

00:16:01.070 --> 00:16:02.810
which defines the
rooting, basically

00:16:02.810 --> 00:16:06.380
match rules that say under
these particular conditions,

00:16:06.380 --> 00:16:09.740
take the traffic and send
it over to these subsets

00:16:09.740 --> 00:16:11.490
and send this much
of the traffic.

00:16:11.490 --> 00:16:14.935
So in practice, a subset of
the yaml looks a bit like this.

00:16:14.935 --> 00:16:16.310
Over on the
destination rule, you

00:16:16.310 --> 00:16:19.190
have a subset called my app.

00:16:19.190 --> 00:16:20.990
Same label selector we used.

00:16:20.990 --> 00:16:23.450
And then over on the
virtual service side,

00:16:23.450 --> 00:16:26.660
you have a route that says
take all the HTTP traffic

00:16:26.660 --> 00:16:30.398
and send it over
to subset my app.

00:16:30.398 --> 00:16:32.440
And so the picture looks
quite similar to what we

00:16:32.440 --> 00:16:33.648
had just now with Kubernetes.

00:16:33.648 --> 00:16:35.517
You've got your
destination rules up there.

00:16:35.517 --> 00:16:36.850
You've got your virtual service.

00:16:36.850 --> 00:16:37.640
It has a subset.

00:16:37.640 --> 00:16:39.760
It's sending all the
traffic over here.

00:16:39.760 --> 00:16:43.150
And now we add the
two new deployments.

00:16:43.150 --> 00:16:44.260
That's outside of Istio.

00:16:44.260 --> 00:16:45.700
That's just regular Kubernetes.

00:16:45.700 --> 00:16:49.990
But now we add this yaml
to our Istio configuration.

00:16:49.990 --> 00:16:51.850
We define two new subsets.

00:16:51.850 --> 00:16:55.520
It's much more legible up there
than it is here, which is nice.

00:16:55.520 --> 00:16:57.850
We define the candidate
in the baseline.

00:16:57.850 --> 00:17:01.150
And we use label selectors to
point specifically at those.

00:17:01.150 --> 00:17:03.910
And then we add a
weight distribution

00:17:03.910 --> 00:17:05.890
over on the virtual
server side, which

00:17:05.890 --> 00:17:08.948
says that we're going to
split the traffic 90-5-5,

00:17:08.948 --> 00:17:09.490
in this case.

00:17:09.490 --> 00:17:12.400
So we end up with a picture that
looks a little bit like this.

00:17:12.400 --> 00:17:14.920
And notice that we can have
a traffic distribution here

00:17:14.920 --> 00:17:16.780
that is different
from the sizing

00:17:16.780 --> 00:17:21.020
of the individual replica
sets, which is really nice.

00:17:21.020 --> 00:17:22.630
So one of the nice
things we can do,

00:17:22.630 --> 00:17:24.940
if you only have 5% of
traffic on your canary

00:17:24.940 --> 00:17:26.680
in your baseline, it
might take a while

00:17:26.680 --> 00:17:30.010
to get enough data to make
a good canary decision.

00:17:30.010 --> 00:17:31.610
You might not want
to wait that long.

00:17:31.610 --> 00:17:33.527
So as you've gained a
little bit of confidence

00:17:33.527 --> 00:17:35.290
that your canary
is doing well, you

00:17:35.290 --> 00:17:38.025
might want to ramp
up the traffic.

00:17:38.025 --> 00:17:39.400
And you can do
that really easily

00:17:39.400 --> 00:17:41.860
with this kind of
configuration change in Istio.

00:17:41.860 --> 00:17:44.230
And then again, without
changing the sizing,

00:17:44.230 --> 00:17:47.900
you now have 80-10-10,
which is nice.

00:17:47.900 --> 00:17:50.320
One important point
to bear in mind here.

00:17:50.320 --> 00:17:52.840
As you decide to ramp up
more and more traffic,

00:17:52.840 --> 00:17:54.250
you pretty much
want to make sure

00:17:54.250 --> 00:17:56.980
that you have a horizontal port
auto scalar on those replica

00:17:56.980 --> 00:17:59.920
sets, because you don't
want them to fall over

00:17:59.920 --> 00:18:02.650
as you said and 50% of
traffic or 40% or whatever,

00:18:02.650 --> 00:18:05.140
to your canary and your
baseline or your candidate

00:18:05.140 --> 00:18:07.460
and your baseline.

00:18:07.460 --> 00:18:09.670
So now if we decide
to go ahead, great.

00:18:09.670 --> 00:18:12.062
We can get rid of the
subset definitions,

00:18:12.062 --> 00:18:14.020
and we can change the
weight distribution back.

00:18:14.020 --> 00:18:16.930
And we can update the
deployment object.

00:18:16.930 --> 00:18:18.230
And then we make that change.

00:18:18.230 --> 00:18:21.610
And as before, it goes through
its regular deployment pattern,

00:18:21.610 --> 00:18:22.510
and we're done.

00:18:22.510 --> 00:18:24.910
We now have version 2 running.

00:18:24.910 --> 00:18:31.640
We've done a nice canary rollout
with Kubernetes and Istio.

00:18:31.640 --> 00:18:34.308
So to add this little kind
of funky advanced topic

00:18:34.308 --> 00:18:34.850
into the mix.

00:18:34.850 --> 00:18:38.150
Imagine we have some
really critical customers.

00:18:38.150 --> 00:18:40.610
We're really, really concerned
that they should not have

00:18:40.610 --> 00:18:41.910
a breaking application on them.

00:18:41.910 --> 00:18:46.760
So we really want to make sure
that when we do a candidate

00:18:46.760 --> 00:18:49.160
evaluation, they don't get
sent to the old version,

00:18:49.160 --> 00:18:50.990
or the new version, rather.

00:18:50.990 --> 00:18:53.510
So we can do content header
based rooting in this case

00:18:53.510 --> 00:18:54.140
to do that.

00:18:54.140 --> 00:18:55.640
I'll skip through
this a little bit.

00:18:55.640 --> 00:18:58.850
You can define a match rule
that matches specifically

00:18:58.850 --> 00:19:00.382
on the header.

00:19:00.382 --> 00:19:02.090
And you can say under
these circumstances

00:19:02.090 --> 00:19:05.260
always send to my old subset.

00:19:05.260 --> 00:19:06.980
Oh, are we missing a slide here?

00:19:06.980 --> 00:19:08.300
No.

00:19:08.300 --> 00:19:10.820
Always send to my
old application.

00:19:10.820 --> 00:19:13.190
And one thing that is
obviously worth bearing in mind

00:19:13.190 --> 00:19:15.380
that if you have these
kind of rules, the traffic

00:19:15.380 --> 00:19:17.380
distribution-- that's
what that little star is--

00:19:17.380 --> 00:19:19.865
changes a little bit
because of course,

00:19:19.865 --> 00:19:21.740
the people that match
this specific condition

00:19:21.740 --> 00:19:24.620
are going to the old version.

00:19:24.620 --> 00:19:28.520
And so you end up with 95-5
of the remaining traffic.

00:19:28.520 --> 00:19:31.940
All right, quick overview
of the recommended pattern

00:19:31.940 --> 00:19:33.380
that we talked about here.

00:19:33.380 --> 00:19:35.142
In the steady
state, you only need

00:19:35.142 --> 00:19:37.100
to keep the running
version of your application

00:19:37.100 --> 00:19:38.720
using a deployment object.

00:19:38.720 --> 00:19:41.240
When you start your rollout,
and you want to do a canary,

00:19:41.240 --> 00:19:44.510
you create two new resources,
a candidate and a baseline.

00:19:44.510 --> 00:19:47.570
You use the deployment
config of the existing

00:19:47.570 --> 00:19:48.630
version for the baseline.

00:19:48.630 --> 00:19:50.005
And you use the
deployment config

00:19:50.005 --> 00:19:52.770
of the new version
for the candidate.

00:19:52.770 --> 00:19:55.910
You send-- you add subsets
to your destination rule,

00:19:55.910 --> 00:19:57.440
and appropriate
weight distribution

00:19:57.440 --> 00:19:59.487
to your virtual service.

00:19:59.487 --> 00:20:01.070
It's useful if you're
automating this,

00:20:01.070 --> 00:20:02.930
if you think of the
weights as parameters

00:20:02.930 --> 00:20:05.540
to your automation scripting
or your automation tool,

00:20:05.540 --> 00:20:08.360
because that makes it easy to
do a multi-step canary that you

00:20:08.360 --> 00:20:11.420
can easily go from
80-10-10 to or 90

00:20:11.420 --> 00:20:14.940
or towards 60-20-20,
something like that.

00:20:14.940 --> 00:20:17.870
And you should definitely use
a Horizontal Pod Autoscaler

00:20:17.870 --> 00:20:19.820
for your baseline
and your candidate,

00:20:19.820 --> 00:20:22.550
so that they ramp up as
the traffic comes up.

00:20:22.550 --> 00:20:26.130
So then you wait, gather
data, and you make a decision.

00:20:26.130 --> 00:20:28.427
If you're good to go
after the canary analysis,

00:20:28.427 --> 00:20:31.010
you just update the deployment
that represents your running up

00:20:31.010 --> 00:20:32.365
and get rid of the rest.

00:20:32.365 --> 00:20:34.490
And you can remove the
subsets from the destination

00:20:34.490 --> 00:20:36.680
rule and the weights
from the virtual service.

00:20:36.680 --> 00:20:38.638
And of course, you can
get rid of the candidate

00:20:38.638 --> 00:20:39.890
and the baseline workloads.

00:20:39.890 --> 00:20:41.805
There's lots of
shortcuts possible here.

00:20:41.805 --> 00:20:44.180
You can, for instance, keep
the candidate in the baseline

00:20:44.180 --> 00:20:46.260
around and have them spun down.

00:20:46.260 --> 00:20:47.907
And that means that
your yaml changes

00:20:47.907 --> 00:20:48.990
might be a little simpler.

00:20:48.990 --> 00:20:51.500
But this is the sort of
cleanest and nicest approach,

00:20:51.500 --> 00:20:53.453
because in your
steady state, you only

00:20:53.453 --> 00:20:55.370
have one running workload,
and everything else

00:20:55.370 --> 00:20:57.920
is ephemeral during the
course of the rollout.

00:20:57.920 --> 00:21:00.263
So, speed up a bit.

00:21:00.263 --> 00:21:02.180
This could be an entire
talk in its own right.

00:21:02.180 --> 00:21:03.560
One thing worth
pointing out here

00:21:03.560 --> 00:21:05.450
is that the rollout
that we looked at

00:21:05.450 --> 00:21:09.090
represents a sequence of
six or seven config changes.

00:21:09.090 --> 00:21:11.090
And if you're thinking
of a get up style pattern

00:21:11.090 --> 00:21:15.050
where you want to capture the
intended state of your runtime

00:21:15.050 --> 00:21:18.680
in a repo, the question becomes
how do you reflect these config

00:21:18.680 --> 00:21:20.780
changes in that repo?

00:21:20.780 --> 00:21:23.360
Clearly, you don't want
to have your users do

00:21:23.360 --> 00:21:25.400
cube-ctl six or
seven times as you're

00:21:25.400 --> 00:21:27.830
doing this canary analysis.

00:21:27.830 --> 00:21:31.280
So automation tooling really
should be updating this repo.

00:21:31.280 --> 00:21:34.700
Another approach that we see
some of the community tooling

00:21:34.700 --> 00:21:37.310
take is to basically hide
this kind of canary pattern

00:21:37.310 --> 00:21:38.660
inside the CRD.

00:21:38.660 --> 00:21:41.030
So you post something
like a canary rollout.

00:21:41.030 --> 00:21:43.190
And it magically makes
all the resources for you

00:21:43.190 --> 00:21:45.250
and runs however long it runs.

00:21:45.250 --> 00:21:47.390
And that's possible, but
one of the things it does

00:21:47.390 --> 00:21:49.700
is it means that your
repository doesn't really

00:21:49.700 --> 00:21:51.590
reflect the actual
state of your cluster

00:21:51.590 --> 00:21:54.550
very well, because you think
you have one deployment,

00:21:54.550 --> 00:21:56.150
but you actually have three.

00:21:56.150 --> 00:21:58.940
And so we think having your
automation tooling make

00:21:58.940 --> 00:22:00.590
these updates back
into your repository

00:22:00.590 --> 00:22:03.710
is a good mix between making
it simple for the user--

00:22:03.710 --> 00:22:05.930
they just need to say
deploy with canary--

00:22:05.930 --> 00:22:07.940
and between having
a representation

00:22:07.940 --> 00:22:09.890
of your actual
cluster state so that

00:22:09.890 --> 00:22:14.090
you can see at any given time
what was really going on.

00:22:14.090 --> 00:22:15.220
A couple more.

00:22:15.220 --> 00:22:17.740
Yes, so a final summary here.

00:22:17.740 --> 00:22:20.110
Three main takeaways
from my side.

00:22:20.110 --> 00:22:23.080
You can implement canary
roll-outs and other strategies

00:22:23.080 --> 00:22:25.150
that go beyond the
vanilla deployment

00:22:25.150 --> 00:22:26.320
object in Kubernetes.

00:22:26.320 --> 00:22:28.930
And you can do that pretty
easily using vanilla Kubernetes

00:22:28.930 --> 00:22:32.440
ID resources or, if you want
more sophistication for canary,

00:22:32.440 --> 00:22:33.880
you can use Istio.

00:22:33.880 --> 00:22:35.770
Add that, and it
gives you two nice--

00:22:35.770 --> 00:22:39.400
at least two nice-- powerful
features including traffic

00:22:39.400 --> 00:22:42.490
shaping and also
content-based routing.

00:22:42.490 --> 00:22:44.620
And then if you want
a recommended sequence

00:22:44.620 --> 00:22:46.690
of config changes
for your resources

00:22:46.690 --> 00:22:48.630
as you want to do
a canary rollout,

00:22:48.630 --> 00:22:50.380
well, we have one in
the slides over here.

00:22:50.380 --> 00:22:53.380
You can easily automate the
set of config changes using

00:22:53.380 --> 00:22:55.270
Spinnaker, as we'll see
right now, or indeed,

00:22:55.270 --> 00:22:56.590
a bunch of other tools.

00:22:56.590 --> 00:22:59.780
And now to actually see what
this looks like for real,

00:22:59.780 --> 00:23:01.161
let's hand it over to Nora.

00:23:01.161 --> 00:23:02.578
NORA LUTZ: Thanks
so much, Andrew.

00:23:02.578 --> 00:23:04.486
[APPLAUSE]

00:23:04.486 --> 00:23:07.625
Awesome.

00:23:07.625 --> 00:23:08.750
A lot of great information.

00:23:08.750 --> 00:23:09.640
Thanks so much.

00:23:09.640 --> 00:23:12.200
So before we get
into our demo here,

00:23:12.200 --> 00:23:14.200
I want to talk
just really quickly

00:23:14.200 --> 00:23:18.100
about who Descartes Labs
is, what we're doing,

00:23:18.100 --> 00:23:21.170
why canary roll-outs are
actually very important to us.

00:23:21.170 --> 00:23:24.160
So at Descartes, we're
focusing on building models

00:23:24.160 --> 00:23:27.790
of the physical world, things
like agriculture, supply

00:23:27.790 --> 00:23:31.690
chains, transportation,
energy production.

00:23:31.690 --> 00:23:33.970
Four years ago, the
founder spun out

00:23:33.970 --> 00:23:36.550
Descartes Labs from the Los
Alamos National Laboratory

00:23:36.550 --> 00:23:39.760
after incubating their
underlying machine learning

00:23:39.760 --> 00:23:42.312
technology for many years.

00:23:42.312 --> 00:23:44.020
So then we took that
and built a software

00:23:44.020 --> 00:23:46.960
as a service platform
on top of GCP

00:23:46.960 --> 00:23:49.750
that our customers can leverage
to build their own machine

00:23:49.750 --> 00:23:52.420
learning models,
utilizing petabytes

00:23:52.420 --> 00:23:55.800
of remote sensing
geospatial data.

00:23:55.800 --> 00:23:59.520
So to comprise the platform,
we use about every GCP product

00:23:59.520 --> 00:24:01.740
available, I think, except
for maybe the exception

00:24:01.740 --> 00:24:03.330
of App Engine and Spanner.

00:24:05.880 --> 00:24:10.750
So here's one quick example
of how we build these models.

00:24:10.750 --> 00:24:15.250
This is a natural color imagery
of farmland in Greenfield, Iowa

00:24:15.250 --> 00:24:17.751
taken from [INAUDIBLE] two.

00:24:17.751 --> 00:24:20.360
And here's that same
imagery after going

00:24:20.360 --> 00:24:21.300
through our pipeline.

00:24:21.300 --> 00:24:24.800
So our platform has empowered
our applied scientists

00:24:24.800 --> 00:24:27.680
to segment up that
imagery into boundaries

00:24:27.680 --> 00:24:30.580
of towns, fields, roads.

00:24:30.580 --> 00:24:33.230
We then took a machine
learning classification model

00:24:33.230 --> 00:24:37.430
on just the fields to show
corn and soy field boundaries.

00:24:37.430 --> 00:24:39.530
And then we took
that a step further,

00:24:39.530 --> 00:24:41.780
and we were able to use the
non-human visible spectrum

00:24:41.780 --> 00:24:45.530
of light and seeing how
travels through the plant

00:24:45.530 --> 00:24:48.010
to see how much fruit
the plant is growing.

00:24:48.010 --> 00:24:51.248
And then we scaled that
up using Kubernetes,

00:24:51.248 --> 00:24:53.540
using hundreds of thousands
of cores, tens of thousands

00:24:53.540 --> 00:24:54.920
of pods.

00:24:54.920 --> 00:24:59.480
So we can view this growth
every single day on every field

00:24:59.480 --> 00:25:02.030
everywhere in the United States.

00:25:02.030 --> 00:25:06.230
So why am I up here talking
about canary releases?

00:25:06.230 --> 00:25:10.550
Obviously Dev Ops is not
our core business model,

00:25:10.550 --> 00:25:14.240
but we need it to always be
improving our feature velocity.

00:25:14.240 --> 00:25:16.080
We need to move faster.

00:25:16.080 --> 00:25:18.980
We need to add new capabilities
and continue scaling

00:25:18.980 --> 00:25:21.520
up our platform.

00:25:21.520 --> 00:25:24.220
But we want to do this
without introducing issues

00:25:24.220 --> 00:25:28.300
that have cascading effects
across our complex interlocking

00:25:28.300 --> 00:25:30.930
services.

00:25:30.930 --> 00:25:32.460
Detecting problems
when new features

00:25:32.460 --> 00:25:36.180
are released to production
is actually pretty hard.

00:25:36.180 --> 00:25:38.430
But what if we could
have a way to detect them

00:25:38.430 --> 00:25:42.150
before they went truly live?

00:25:42.150 --> 00:25:44.640
And we wanted to
do all of this with

00:25:44.640 --> 00:25:46.950
minimal human intervention.

00:25:46.950 --> 00:25:48.630
Humans are good.

00:25:48.630 --> 00:25:50.100
Humans are great.

00:25:50.100 --> 00:25:52.320
Engineers with lots of
operational experience

00:25:52.320 --> 00:25:56.100
are wonderful, but
they're not infallible.

00:25:56.100 --> 00:25:58.410
To be able to sustainably
meet our goals,

00:25:58.410 --> 00:26:02.438
we need a nuanced and
fully automated approach.

00:26:02.438 --> 00:26:04.230
So these are some of
the circumstances that

00:26:04.230 --> 00:26:08.100
led us to investigate
tools to help us do things

00:26:08.100 --> 00:26:10.968
like canary analysis
and canary deployments.

00:26:10.968 --> 00:26:13.260
What we found when we were
trying to solve this problem

00:26:13.260 --> 00:26:15.790
can be split the
three main categories.

00:26:15.790 --> 00:26:17.460
I'll briefly go over those.

00:26:17.460 --> 00:26:20.370
We looked at things like
continuous delivery.

00:26:20.370 --> 00:26:25.050
We looked at tools like GitLab,
which is a very mature delivery

00:26:25.050 --> 00:26:28.835
system that required a lot of
buying into their ecosystem.

00:26:28.835 --> 00:26:30.210
We also looked at
a lot of things

00:26:30.210 --> 00:26:33.600
like jenkins
scripts, which seemed

00:26:33.600 --> 00:26:35.180
a bit heavyweight
for our use case,

00:26:35.180 --> 00:26:38.113
kind of like bringing a chainsaw
where we needed a scalpel,

00:26:38.113 --> 00:26:39.780
and also, the story
to implement carries

00:26:39.780 --> 00:26:43.350
was not as clear at the
time we looked at it.

00:26:43.350 --> 00:26:46.407
And then finally, we
looked at Spinnaker,

00:26:46.407 --> 00:26:48.990
which is an open source project
spun out on Netflix, for those

00:26:48.990 --> 00:26:50.390
not familiar.

00:26:50.390 --> 00:26:53.640
It has a feature-rich
Kubernetes v2 provider

00:26:53.640 --> 00:26:57.780
that supports complex pipelines,
consuming and producing

00:26:57.780 --> 00:27:00.050
Kubernetes native objects.

00:27:00.050 --> 00:27:02.665
And hopefully, it also
supports canary analysis

00:27:02.665 --> 00:27:04.040
through something
called Kayenta.

00:27:06.660 --> 00:27:10.308
And then next, we're looking
to get those metrics to be

00:27:10.308 --> 00:27:11.600
able to do our canary analysis.

00:27:11.600 --> 00:27:14.240
We need to be able to do those
fine grain traffic splits.

00:27:14.240 --> 00:27:18.980
So, lucky for us, we're already
using Istio in production.

00:27:18.980 --> 00:27:21.813
So we didn't have to
look very far there.

00:27:21.813 --> 00:27:24.230
And then finally, when we
looked how to actually implement

00:27:24.230 --> 00:27:29.120
this in practice, how to
do these canary analyses,

00:27:29.120 --> 00:27:31.010
we actually, at the time
we looked last year,

00:27:31.010 --> 00:27:35.660
didn't find a very consistent
pattern or best practices

00:27:35.660 --> 00:27:38.277
guidelines as reported
by the community.

00:27:38.277 --> 00:27:39.860
In fact, what we did
find at that time

00:27:39.860 --> 00:27:44.730
was a lot of
conflicting approaches.

00:27:44.730 --> 00:27:46.910
And so of course,
here today I'm going

00:27:46.910 --> 00:27:51.660
to introduce one more approach
for you all to consider.

00:27:51.660 --> 00:27:53.345
So here's what we
use at Descartes

00:27:53.345 --> 00:27:57.382
to power continuous deployment
pipelines with canaries.

00:27:57.382 --> 00:27:59.090
My hope is that you're
going to take away

00:27:59.090 --> 00:28:01.730
some ideas and
concepts on how this

00:28:01.730 --> 00:28:04.290
can work at your organizations.

00:28:04.290 --> 00:28:08.930
So we have Spinnaker with
Kayenta of a canary analysis.

00:28:08.930 --> 00:28:10.420
We have Kubernetes.

00:28:10.420 --> 00:28:13.010
Our Kubernetes all run on GKE.

00:28:13.010 --> 00:28:16.180
And then finally, we have Istio.

00:28:16.180 --> 00:28:18.067
So before I hop into
the demo, I want

00:28:18.067 --> 00:28:19.900
to show you where we're
starting from, like,

00:28:19.900 --> 00:28:23.080
what is the current
state of affairs?

00:28:23.080 --> 00:28:26.070
We have our service here tiles.

00:28:26.070 --> 00:28:27.940
This is a real
service that we use

00:28:27.940 --> 00:28:31.000
to scale up analysis over
large geographic areas

00:28:31.000 --> 00:28:35.860
by splitting them into a
grid of smaller sized tiles.

00:28:35.860 --> 00:28:39.970
We have our deployment
object, our service object.

00:28:39.970 --> 00:28:42.470
And then we have our Istio
virtual service tiles release,

00:28:42.470 --> 00:28:44.230
which routes all of
our production traffic

00:28:44.230 --> 00:28:46.393
to our Kubernetes service.

00:28:46.393 --> 00:28:48.310
And then finally, we
have our ingress gateway,

00:28:48.310 --> 00:28:50.352
which is receiving and
routing all of our traffic

00:28:50.352 --> 00:28:53.230
coming into our service mesh.

00:28:53.230 --> 00:28:55.120
So what we're going
to try and do today

00:28:55.120 --> 00:28:59.250
is update live to production
a version two of this app

00:28:59.250 --> 00:29:01.140
right here on stage.

00:29:01.140 --> 00:29:03.030
So let's go ahead
and head to the demo.

00:29:05.934 --> 00:29:09.180
All right, perfect.

00:29:09.180 --> 00:29:11.830
So here we have
our Spinnaker UI.

00:29:11.830 --> 00:29:14.070
Now don't worry if you
haven't seen this before.

00:29:14.070 --> 00:29:16.097
It's a lot going on.

00:29:16.097 --> 00:29:18.180
And come talk to me after
if you want to talk more

00:29:18.180 --> 00:29:19.263
about the details of this.

00:29:21.817 --> 00:29:23.400
And we're going to
spend a lot of time

00:29:23.400 --> 00:29:26.670
talking about the
intricacies of configuring

00:29:26.670 --> 00:29:27.710
canaries for analysis.

00:29:27.710 --> 00:29:30.210
So I'm not going to spend a lot
of time on Spinnaker concept

00:29:30.210 --> 00:29:35.010
as much as how you fine
tune your canary analysis.

00:29:35.010 --> 00:29:36.900
Because it's not just magic.

00:29:36.900 --> 00:29:40.290
When I pull back the curtain,
it's actually pretty nuanced.

00:29:40.290 --> 00:29:42.720
So anyway, let's move on.

00:29:42.720 --> 00:29:44.250
In our case, we're
using the version

00:29:44.250 --> 00:29:48.000
two provider, which allows us
to manage many of our Kubernetes

00:29:48.000 --> 00:29:52.385
kinds, deployments being one
of those, and also Istio kinds.

00:29:52.385 --> 00:29:53.760
Most relevantly
to our discussion

00:29:53.760 --> 00:29:56.790
here is going to be
our virtual service.

00:29:56.790 --> 00:30:00.270
We have our application
canary, which isn't actually

00:30:00.270 --> 00:30:02.310
a one to one mapping
with tiles, but it

00:30:02.310 --> 00:30:04.470
does contain our
pipeline that we're

00:30:04.470 --> 00:30:08.630
concerned about here, which is
our next demo canary pipeline.

00:30:08.630 --> 00:30:12.490
So if we click through, we can
see that our pipeline contains

00:30:12.490 --> 00:30:16.160
artifacts, parameters, and
stages, stages consumed

00:30:16.160 --> 00:30:17.900
as artifacts and parameters.

00:30:17.900 --> 00:30:21.310
Some of them are
canary analysis stages.

00:30:21.310 --> 00:30:23.570
And in our case--

00:30:23.570 --> 00:30:25.950
let's go here--
the artifacts we're

00:30:25.950 --> 00:30:27.910
concerned with for the
tile service itself.

00:30:27.910 --> 00:30:31.850
So we have our deployment
manifest captured in GitHub.

00:30:31.850 --> 00:30:34.470
We have our virtual service.

00:30:34.470 --> 00:30:37.380
And then finally, we have our
tiles image, which is in GCR.

00:30:41.360 --> 00:30:44.040
So let's finally
just jump into it.

00:30:44.040 --> 00:30:46.030
Let's kick off the pipeline.

00:30:46.030 --> 00:30:48.660
I'm going to start a
manual execution here.

00:30:48.660 --> 00:30:51.350
And I'm going to supply
an image tag, which

00:30:51.350 --> 00:30:55.880
of course, is now not copied,
because my computer turned off.

00:30:55.880 --> 00:30:56.900
Thank you, demagods.

00:31:02.370 --> 00:31:06.480
So we supplied this
as a parameter to bind

00:31:06.480 --> 00:31:10.210
to our correct image in GCR
in our deployment manifest.

00:31:10.210 --> 00:31:13.390
Normally, this pipeline will
be triggered by a Pub/Sub.

00:31:13.390 --> 00:31:15.070
So when a new tag
is made available,

00:31:15.070 --> 00:31:16.812
the tag would then
be sent to Spinnaker

00:31:16.812 --> 00:31:17.770
as part of the payload.

00:31:20.305 --> 00:31:22.180
So let's talk a little
bit about these stages

00:31:22.180 --> 00:31:24.680
as they start to run through.

00:31:24.680 --> 00:31:27.530
We have our get image from
production stage, which

00:31:27.530 --> 00:31:31.940
produces an artifact, namely
our tiles image that's currently

00:31:31.940 --> 00:31:34.960
running in our tiles released
deployment version one.

00:31:34.960 --> 00:31:36.720
So this is what
Andrew talked about.

00:31:36.720 --> 00:31:38.270
You need to get
the images running

00:31:38.270 --> 00:31:41.430
in production to be able to
feed that to your baseline.

00:31:41.430 --> 00:31:43.100
So that's a pretty
critical moment.

00:31:43.100 --> 00:31:45.840
This is going to be
consumed on the next stage.

00:31:45.840 --> 00:31:49.670
So now we have our canaries and
our baseline being deployed.

00:31:49.670 --> 00:31:52.220
Canary is going to be deployed
with the candidate release

00:31:52.220 --> 00:31:55.310
image tag, baseline
with the image tag

00:31:55.310 --> 00:31:58.410
that we obtain from
our previous stage.

00:31:58.410 --> 00:32:01.560
Something to note here,
baselines and canaries

00:32:01.560 --> 00:32:05.340
are using identical Horizontal
Pod Autoscalers, deployments,

00:32:05.340 --> 00:32:07.320
and service objects.

00:32:07.320 --> 00:32:11.880
So this helps us eliminate
variations that could affect

00:32:11.880 --> 00:32:13.185
adversely analysis results.

00:32:16.680 --> 00:32:19.750
So, once those finish
deploying, our virtual service

00:32:19.750 --> 00:32:21.500
is going to be updated
for the first time.

00:32:24.340 --> 00:32:26.620
To shift a percentage
of production traffic

00:32:26.620 --> 00:32:30.050
away from the production release
and towards the canary baseline

00:32:30.050 --> 00:32:30.550
pair--

00:32:30.550 --> 00:32:34.460
So we're going to start
by shifting 10% of traffic

00:32:34.460 --> 00:32:36.490
to each the canary
and baseline services,

00:32:36.490 --> 00:32:40.090
and keep 80% running
to our release.

00:32:40.090 --> 00:32:42.070
We have several
stages in our pipeline

00:32:42.070 --> 00:32:44.980
that manipulate the virtual
service for tiles release.

00:32:44.980 --> 00:32:48.040
So we increase the weighting as
we go so more and more traffic

00:32:48.040 --> 00:32:50.440
heads to canary and baseline.

00:32:50.440 --> 00:32:53.170
Something I think it's
worth pointing out here

00:32:53.170 --> 00:32:55.960
is that instead of maintaining
versioning of virtual service

00:32:55.960 --> 00:33:00.220
manifest for each and every
of these combinations,

00:33:00.220 --> 00:33:02.320
we're parameterizing this
using something called

00:33:02.320 --> 00:33:03.790
Spinnaker Expressions.

00:33:03.790 --> 00:33:06.040
And these are going to be
evaluated during each stage.

00:33:09.670 --> 00:33:11.165
So let's go back here and--

00:33:15.900 --> 00:33:16.400
all right.

00:33:16.400 --> 00:33:22.938
So at this point our virtual
service is then [INAUDIBLE] out

00:33:22.938 --> 00:33:23.480
and deployed.

00:33:26.018 --> 00:33:28.310
And we're going to move on
to our first canary analysis

00:33:28.310 --> 00:33:31.010
stage.

00:33:31.010 --> 00:33:32.990
So this stage is
powered by Kayenta.

00:33:32.990 --> 00:33:35.860
This is a canary analysis
service released jointly

00:33:35.860 --> 00:33:39.370
by Google and Netflix that
aggregates and compares

00:33:39.370 --> 00:33:43.450
metrics for judgment whether
a canary is healthy relative

00:33:43.450 --> 00:33:45.280
to a baseline.

00:33:45.280 --> 00:33:47.710
As I mentioned, this is now
integrated into Spinnaker,

00:33:47.710 --> 00:33:48.680
and can be enabled.

00:33:48.680 --> 00:33:50.570
I'm not going to
go into that here.

00:33:50.570 --> 00:33:53.380
But it's fully documented.

00:33:53.380 --> 00:33:55.820
And we're happy to
talk about that later.

00:33:55.820 --> 00:33:57.940
So there's two main
areas of configuration

00:33:57.940 --> 00:33:59.692
when it comes to
canary analysis.

00:33:59.692 --> 00:34:01.150
And the first is
our canary config.

00:34:03.850 --> 00:34:06.850
So in our canary config,
we're to specify metrics

00:34:06.850 --> 00:34:11.120
that we want to compare between
a baseline and a canary.

00:34:11.120 --> 00:34:14.150
Not shown here, but actually
very relevant to this,

00:34:14.150 --> 00:34:16.219
is our telemetry provider.

00:34:16.219 --> 00:34:21.199
We can't compare anything if we
don't have metric information.

00:34:21.199 --> 00:34:22.750
In this case, our
Istio metrics are

00:34:22.750 --> 00:34:25.514
being exported to Stackdriver.

00:34:25.514 --> 00:34:27.639
So we're going to use
Stackdriver for our use case,

00:34:27.639 --> 00:34:31.179
but you can also use things
like Prometheus, Datadog,

00:34:31.179 --> 00:34:33.580
and others.

00:34:33.580 --> 00:34:36.350
So what key metric do we
actually care about for tiles?

00:34:36.350 --> 00:34:38.460
What do we want to
analyze when we're trying

00:34:38.460 --> 00:34:42.650
to add a new candidate release?

00:34:42.650 --> 00:34:44.480
Response latency
to client requests

00:34:44.480 --> 00:34:47.060
is one metric that's
pretty important.

00:34:47.060 --> 00:34:49.760
We want retrieval of
tiles of rasterized data

00:34:49.760 --> 00:34:53.120
to be quick, so as not to
impede higher level analysis.

00:34:56.670 --> 00:35:00.140
So let's go ahead and
click into that metric.

00:35:00.140 --> 00:35:02.620
We should think of these
metrics as a set of templates

00:35:02.620 --> 00:35:05.230
that for queries, they're
going to execute it

00:35:05.230 --> 00:35:08.770
against your telemetry
store for both

00:35:08.770 --> 00:35:11.830
your baseline and your canary.

00:35:11.830 --> 00:35:13.900
We're going to define
failure depending

00:35:13.900 --> 00:35:17.560
on whether the canary metric is
seen to increase statistically

00:35:17.560 --> 00:35:20.090
relative to our baseline.

00:35:20.090 --> 00:35:22.850
In this case, we want to fail
their response latency is

00:35:22.850 --> 00:35:26.800
higher coming from the canary.

00:35:26.800 --> 00:35:30.010
We chose a resource type that
represents a set of time series

00:35:30.010 --> 00:35:35.320
data that we want to measure,
in this case Kubernetes pod.

00:35:35.320 --> 00:35:37.690
We're going to need to
choose a metric type that has

00:35:37.690 --> 00:35:39.580
data specific to this resource.

00:35:39.580 --> 00:35:42.280
So Istio exposes all sorts
of great metrics right out

00:35:42.280 --> 00:35:43.720
of the box that you can use.

00:35:43.720 --> 00:35:46.900
In our case, we're measuring
client round latencies.

00:35:46.900 --> 00:35:49.750
And so this is a distribution
of latency values.

00:35:52.860 --> 00:35:56.390
We then want to use things
like grouping and filtering

00:35:56.390 --> 00:35:58.460
to further refine
date these data points

00:35:58.460 --> 00:36:00.870
that you want to compare.

00:36:00.870 --> 00:36:03.240
Being able to get that
exactly the right data

00:36:03.240 --> 00:36:05.880
that you want to compare is
the biggest part of the nuance

00:36:05.880 --> 00:36:07.280
here.

00:36:07.280 --> 00:36:10.550
So we're going to filter
out unhealthy responses--

00:36:10.550 --> 00:36:13.070
anything not a 200
series response.

00:36:13.070 --> 00:36:15.598
In other metrics you may care
very well very much about

00:36:15.598 --> 00:36:17.390
whether or not it's
healthier or unhealthy.

00:36:17.390 --> 00:36:22.340
But in this case, we want to
consider healthy responses.

00:36:22.340 --> 00:36:24.400
We want to filter on
the destination workload

00:36:24.400 --> 00:36:28.630
name, which is going to resolve
to either tiles base line

00:36:28.630 --> 00:36:29.660
or tiles canary.

00:36:29.660 --> 00:36:32.490
Remember, these are templates.

00:36:32.490 --> 00:36:38.452
Where our namespace is
tiles, and request is a post.

00:36:38.452 --> 00:36:40.160
Because in this
particular execution path

00:36:40.160 --> 00:36:42.035
that we're testing,
receives post requests.

00:36:44.463 --> 00:36:46.380
And then we're going to
take that and actually

00:36:46.380 --> 00:36:49.078
clean that up and aggregate
that a little further.

00:36:49.078 --> 00:36:51.620
We're going to help smooth out
this distribution for a better

00:36:51.620 --> 00:36:55.100
comparison using aggregators,
aligners, and reducers.

00:36:55.100 --> 00:36:57.020
So we want to choose
the 50th percentile

00:36:57.020 --> 00:36:59.690
value of each
latency distribution

00:36:59.690 --> 00:37:04.060
as a single data point for the
whole period of one minute.

00:37:04.060 --> 00:37:06.612
And why do we do this?

00:37:06.612 --> 00:37:08.070
One of the reasons
we do this is we

00:37:08.070 --> 00:37:11.970
want to safeguard in our
analysis against things

00:37:11.970 --> 00:37:15.930
like a random expensive request
or a series of those, network

00:37:15.930 --> 00:37:17.820
failures, things like that.

00:37:17.820 --> 00:37:20.050
They don't creep in
and accidentally cause

00:37:20.050 --> 00:37:21.300
false negatives and positives.

00:37:21.300 --> 00:37:24.120
So by aggregating
this data, you're

00:37:24.120 --> 00:37:26.961
going to be able to have a more
apples to apples comparison.

00:37:29.610 --> 00:37:31.460
So let's go back here.

00:37:31.460 --> 00:37:34.370
If you do choose to analyze
more than one metric, which

00:37:34.370 --> 00:37:37.970
is probably recommended,
these can be grouped.

00:37:37.970 --> 00:37:40.170
And those groups can be
weighted in importance.

00:37:40.170 --> 00:37:42.170
One example of why you
might want to do this--

00:37:42.170 --> 00:37:44.510
in our case, we chose
latency, but if we also

00:37:44.510 --> 00:37:49.700
had error rate as one of our
metrics, in this scenario,

00:37:49.700 --> 00:37:52.130
latency metric analysis
shows that it's

00:37:52.130 --> 00:37:54.840
near identical performance
between canary and baseline.

00:37:54.840 --> 00:37:57.400
And so the analysis
is successful,

00:37:57.400 --> 00:38:00.722
but error rate is significantly
higher in the canary.

00:38:00.722 --> 00:38:02.180
So they should
probably be weighted

00:38:02.180 --> 00:38:05.270
higher as it has much
more severe implications

00:38:05.270 --> 00:38:09.930
for your service health and
for your customer experience.

00:38:09.930 --> 00:38:13.110
And then finally, I'll briefly
touch on scoring thresholds.

00:38:13.110 --> 00:38:15.680
So our marginal score
is the moon value

00:38:15.680 --> 00:38:18.230
that a canary analysis can
have and still proceed on

00:38:18.230 --> 00:38:20.480
to further analyses.

00:38:20.480 --> 00:38:22.910
And our path score is
the minimum score value

00:38:22.910 --> 00:38:25.460
that the final analysis
should have in order

00:38:25.460 --> 00:38:27.740
to pass for the entire stage.

00:38:27.740 --> 00:38:30.030
In our case, we're only
measuring one metric.

00:38:30.030 --> 00:38:32.150
So it's kind of a
binary operation.

00:38:32.150 --> 00:38:35.960
Either you failed,
0, or passed, 100.

00:38:35.960 --> 00:38:39.757
So we set our metric, our scores
to 99 and 100 respectively.

00:38:45.830 --> 00:38:47.920
So the second piece
of this puzzle

00:38:47.920 --> 00:38:51.000
is the analysis stage itself.

00:38:51.000 --> 00:38:53.610
In the stage config
we want to specify

00:38:53.610 --> 00:38:56.280
how Kayenta is going to operate
for this particular analysis

00:38:56.280 --> 00:38:58.870
stage.

00:38:58.870 --> 00:39:02.490
We're going to set our
analysis to real time

00:39:02.490 --> 00:39:04.740
to measure our deployments
that are running right now.

00:39:04.740 --> 00:39:06.880
But you could also set
this to retrospective.

00:39:06.880 --> 00:39:08.380
So if you have a
canary and baseline

00:39:08.380 --> 00:39:11.380
pair that have been running
in the past days, past weeks,

00:39:11.380 --> 00:39:16.420
past months even, you could have
this do the exact same analysis

00:39:16.420 --> 00:39:18.060
over that period of time.

00:39:18.060 --> 00:39:21.300
But for this demo,
we set real time.

00:39:21.300 --> 00:39:22.590
We set our config name.

00:39:22.590 --> 00:39:25.295
But I think the most
important value to set here,

00:39:25.295 --> 00:39:26.670
the most important
thing to think

00:39:26.670 --> 00:39:29.220
about is the lifetime value.

00:39:29.220 --> 00:39:31.420
Currently, we have it
set to three minutes.

00:39:31.420 --> 00:39:32.380
Not very long time.

00:39:32.380 --> 00:39:34.010
That's what this demo.

00:39:34.010 --> 00:39:36.390
And I'll go into why
this is so important.

00:39:36.390 --> 00:39:39.930
In order for your comparison
to be accurate and not

00:39:39.930 --> 00:39:43.110
reflect more potential false
negatives or positives,

00:39:43.110 --> 00:39:44.730
you want to set a
long enough lifetime

00:39:44.730 --> 00:39:48.300
to gather enough data points
to ensure that anomalies

00:39:48.300 --> 00:39:50.080
don't skew your analysis.

00:39:50.080 --> 00:39:54.880
So you want to set a long
time to gather this data.

00:39:54.880 --> 00:39:56.880
We also set our base line
and a canary namespace

00:39:56.880 --> 00:39:57.870
and deployments here.

00:40:03.730 --> 00:40:08.940
So after our analysis
run is complete,

00:40:08.940 --> 00:40:12.935
we can view our analysis
report once Spinnaker loads.

00:40:16.300 --> 00:40:19.120
So in this amplitude
versus time chart--

00:40:19.120 --> 00:40:23.210
let me zoom out a little bit, so
you can see all of this at once

00:40:23.210 --> 00:40:25.030
hopefully.

00:40:25.030 --> 00:40:27.160
In this amplitude
versus time chart,

00:40:27.160 --> 00:40:28.910
you can that the
comparison of time series

00:40:28.910 --> 00:40:31.410
data we filtered out from
our Istio client round trip

00:40:31.410 --> 00:40:33.500
latency as metric.

00:40:33.500 --> 00:40:35.840
So it looks like
our canary is not

00:40:35.840 --> 00:40:37.850
introducing statistically
significant higher

00:40:37.850 --> 00:40:40.300
latency than the baseline.

00:40:40.300 --> 00:40:44.670
And so our analysis is
successful, which is great.

00:40:44.670 --> 00:40:47.895
You can also view things
like the maximum minimum,

00:40:47.895 --> 00:40:49.520
and average latency
data points gleaned

00:40:49.520 --> 00:40:51.860
from our 50th
percentile time series.

00:40:51.860 --> 00:40:54.920
And if you actually click
through here to your metrics,

00:40:54.920 --> 00:40:56.540
you can see all of
your data points

00:40:56.540 --> 00:40:58.860
that were gathered
throughout the lifetime.

00:40:58.860 --> 00:41:01.780
But you're probably
thinking, well Nora,

00:41:01.780 --> 00:41:03.370
this doesn't look
very impressive.

00:41:03.370 --> 00:41:05.140
It's three data points.

00:41:05.140 --> 00:41:07.420
And this actually
highlights one of the issues

00:41:07.420 --> 00:41:09.210
with such a short lifetime.

00:41:09.210 --> 00:41:13.190
With a period of one minute and
an analysis lifetime of three,

00:41:13.190 --> 00:41:16.060
that only allows for three
data points to be aggregated.

00:41:16.060 --> 00:41:18.340
With such a small
sample size, analysis

00:41:18.340 --> 00:41:20.860
results maybe less
than representative.

00:41:20.860 --> 00:41:24.010
So it's very key that you
spend enough time gathering

00:41:24.010 --> 00:41:28.000
this data, on the order of hours
and days, not just minutes.

00:41:33.220 --> 00:41:36.922
So, now that our
analysis is successful,

00:41:36.922 --> 00:41:37.880
we're going to move on.

00:41:37.880 --> 00:41:40.750
The pipeline is going to move
on and increase the weights

00:41:40.750 --> 00:41:42.030
to our canary baseline pair.

00:41:44.720 --> 00:41:48.940
Let's go ahead and head back
to the slides real fast.

00:41:48.940 --> 00:41:51.550
So, at this point, we're
starting to adjust our weights.

00:41:54.190 --> 00:41:55.870
We're updating that
virtual service

00:41:55.870 --> 00:41:59.530
to set our traffic
to 20, 20, and 60.

00:41:59.530 --> 00:42:04.490
And as this occurs,
our traffic and thus,

00:42:04.490 --> 00:42:07.215
the CPU across each deployment
is going to increase.

00:42:07.215 --> 00:42:08.590
And so our horizontal
[INAUDIBLE]

00:42:08.590 --> 00:42:13.870
scale is going to begin to scale
our baseline and tiles release

00:42:13.870 --> 00:42:15.320
as a function of the load.

00:42:15.320 --> 00:42:18.040
One of the ways to visualize
this is with Stackdriver.

00:42:18.040 --> 00:42:21.130
Here's a chart I created
to view the current numbers

00:42:21.130 --> 00:42:27.850
of tiles pods as reflected
by their CPU request counts.

00:42:27.850 --> 00:42:30.220
You can see our release
deployment scaling up and down

00:42:30.220 --> 00:42:33.430
as a function of traffic, while
our canary and baseline are

00:42:33.430 --> 00:42:37.030
also scaling up to meet the
demand as virtual service

00:42:37.030 --> 00:42:39.690
weights are increased.

00:42:39.690 --> 00:42:41.762
So, eventually our
virtual service

00:42:41.762 --> 00:42:43.470
will have weighted
the canary in baseline

00:42:43.470 --> 00:42:46.230
to receive 80% of traffic.

00:42:46.230 --> 00:42:48.678
So we're pretty
confident at this point

00:42:48.678 --> 00:42:49.845
we've got a healthy release.

00:42:52.580 --> 00:42:55.080
And then we're going to perform
the final analysis currently

00:42:55.080 --> 00:42:57.960
using our same config and
stage parameters as before.

00:42:57.960 --> 00:43:01.140
The reason you might
do multiple analyses

00:43:01.140 --> 00:43:04.050
is because behavior is going
to change as your candidate

00:43:04.050 --> 00:43:05.460
scales up.

00:43:05.460 --> 00:43:08.640
As you scale, it's going to
introduce potential issues,

00:43:08.640 --> 00:43:11.550
things like memory
leaks or a slow burning

00:43:11.550 --> 00:43:13.270
bug, that sort of thing.

00:43:13.270 --> 00:43:15.990
So having multiple
periods as you scale

00:43:15.990 --> 00:43:18.600
is going to give you more
confidence that your candidate

00:43:18.600 --> 00:43:19.500
is actually healthy.

00:43:23.730 --> 00:43:25.940
So if that's successful,
our release deployment

00:43:25.940 --> 00:43:30.110
is updated to use our new
tiles image tag, version 2.

00:43:30.110 --> 00:43:33.320
And then we're going to begin
reversing this weighting

00:43:33.320 --> 00:43:36.170
process, letting our
tiles release deployment

00:43:36.170 --> 00:43:38.390
scale back up gradually
as it receives more

00:43:38.390 --> 00:43:39.830
and more of the traffic again.

00:43:39.830 --> 00:43:42.720
This is so we don't shock
the system so to speak.

00:43:42.720 --> 00:43:45.590
As our release has scaled
down, if we reintroduced

00:43:45.590 --> 00:43:49.190
100% of traffic, that could
have a spike from latencies,

00:43:49.190 --> 00:43:52.530
failures,
unpredictable behavior.

00:43:52.530 --> 00:43:55.370
So we want to start scaling
down our baseline first,

00:43:55.370 --> 00:43:58.670
because this is at the oldest
deployment at this point,

00:43:58.670 --> 00:44:02.420
and scaling up our
production release.

00:44:02.420 --> 00:44:04.910
Then we scale down
the canary until we

00:44:04.910 --> 00:44:07.190
have our successful release.

00:44:07.190 --> 00:44:10.100
It's important to note
that regardless of success

00:44:10.100 --> 00:44:13.190
or failure, the canary and
baseline deployments are

00:44:13.190 --> 00:44:16.760
deleted, and tiles released
is reset to receive

00:44:16.760 --> 00:44:20.775
100% of traffic via
our virtual service.

00:44:20.775 --> 00:44:22.400
This is implemented
by another pipeline

00:44:22.400 --> 00:44:24.740
that's triggered after
the previous is completed

00:44:24.740 --> 00:44:27.180
or failed.

00:44:27.180 --> 00:44:29.610
So to wrap up, here
are some pro tips

00:44:29.610 --> 00:44:31.410
I can give you guys
when you're looking

00:44:31.410 --> 00:44:33.510
to implement this yourself.

00:44:33.510 --> 00:44:37.260
Choose metrics that reflect
key service health indicators.

00:44:37.260 --> 00:44:40.500
So if your candidate release
was to go into production,

00:44:40.500 --> 00:44:42.270
choose what you would
care to measure.

00:44:42.270 --> 00:44:46.160
If you already have things
like service level indicators,

00:44:46.160 --> 00:44:47.660
have those established.

00:44:47.660 --> 00:44:50.450
It's a great starting place.

00:44:50.450 --> 00:44:52.400
Accruing a large
enough sample size

00:44:52.400 --> 00:44:55.220
and aligning the data for
apples to apples comparisons

00:44:55.220 --> 00:44:59.600
is critical to avoid false
negatives and false positives.

00:44:59.600 --> 00:45:01.520
Having identical
baselines carries

00:45:01.520 --> 00:45:04.430
and releases, except
for image tag,

00:45:04.430 --> 00:45:07.820
helps ensure that you're
examining the right difference

00:45:07.820 --> 00:45:09.590
between your service instances.

00:45:09.590 --> 00:45:13.690
Change one variable
for your analysis.

00:45:13.690 --> 00:45:15.280
And I didn't go
over it very much,

00:45:15.280 --> 00:45:19.270
but every deployment Horizontal
Pod Autoscaler, service

00:45:19.270 --> 00:45:21.810
and virtual service
was fully parameterized

00:45:21.810 --> 00:45:23.210
with Spinnaker expressions.

00:45:23.210 --> 00:45:24.835
This meant I can
avoid having diversion

00:45:24.835 --> 00:45:28.060
of potentially hundreds
or thousands of manifests,

00:45:28.060 --> 00:45:30.980
and dynamically
populate them at runtime

00:45:30.980 --> 00:45:32.632
with Spinnaker parameters.

00:45:32.632 --> 00:45:34.590
This is a really powerful
feature of Spinnaker,

00:45:34.590 --> 00:45:38.840
and should absolutely
be taking advantage of.

00:45:38.840 --> 00:45:42.095
And then finally,
the way Kayenta

00:45:42.095 --> 00:45:44.220
interprets these metrics
labels some Stackdriver is

00:45:44.220 --> 00:45:45.780
a little bit opaque.

00:45:45.780 --> 00:45:47.480
So since we're
using Stackdriver,

00:45:47.480 --> 00:45:49.530
I personally found
that using the metrics

00:45:49.530 --> 00:45:52.927
explorer very, very helpful
in teasing out those mappings.

00:45:52.927 --> 00:45:55.260
So taking advantage of the
tools that your metrics store

00:45:55.260 --> 00:45:59.180
provides to help
you get this right.

00:45:59.180 --> 00:46:01.375
So canary analysis
and CD for Kubernetes

00:46:01.375 --> 00:46:03.230
is an ever-moving target.

00:46:03.230 --> 00:46:05.930
We want to work with you
guys, the community, to help

00:46:05.930 --> 00:46:07.855
shape these patterns and tools.

00:46:07.855 --> 00:46:09.980
I'd recommend checking out
the Spinnaker Kubernetes

00:46:09.980 --> 00:46:12.680
special interest group and
the hashtag Kubernetes channel

00:46:12.680 --> 00:46:15.630
on Spinnaker Slack to join in.

00:46:15.630 --> 00:46:18.080
And then finally, a little plug.

00:46:18.080 --> 00:46:20.545
Come check us out at
DescartesLabs.com.

00:46:20.545 --> 00:46:22.670
If you're interested in
using our platform to power

00:46:22.670 --> 00:46:24.500
your geospatial machine
learning models,

00:46:24.500 --> 00:46:26.930
or just curious about
what we have to offer,

00:46:26.930 --> 00:46:30.780
check out our API's at
docs.DescartesLabs.com.

00:46:30.780 --> 00:46:33.170
And if you also have a
love for geospatial data,

00:46:33.170 --> 00:46:35.450
modeling the earth, want
to solve tough engineering

00:46:35.450 --> 00:46:39.040
challenges, check our Jobs
page or just shoot us an email

00:46:39.040 --> 00:46:43.230
at hello@DescartesLabs.com.

00:46:43.230 --> 00:46:43.730
All right.

00:46:43.730 --> 00:46:45.063
ANDREW PHILIPS: Thank you, Nora.

